    I = infected
    R = recovered

    t=0:   S S I S S
    t=1:   S I I S S
    t=2:   I I R I S
    t=3:   I R R I I

Probabilistic infection and recovery drive the dynamics.

FORMAL CHARACTER
PCA are:

    * local (finite neighborhood)
    * shift-invariant (same kernel everywhere)
    * synchronous (parallel update)
    * stochastic (probability kernels)
    * Markovian (memoryless except for current configuration)

They sit at the intersection of:

    - symbolic dynamics
    - interacting particle systems
    - nonequilibrium statistical mechanics

PHASE TRANSITIONS & PERCOLATION
------------------------------------------------------------------
   * CA can exhibit critical behavior similar to physical systems.
   * Directed percolation appears as the canonical universality class
     for absorbing-state transitions in PCA.
   * Critical exponents, scaling laws, and threshold behavior are
     discussed in the context of CA models.

CORE 
Directed percolation is a stochastic process describing how "activity"
spreads through space *and* time with a preferred direction (usually
time). Each site can be:

    ACTIVE   (can spread activity)
    INACTIVE (cannot spread; may be absorbing)

The system undergoes a phase transition between:

    - SURVIVAL (activity persists indefinitely)
    - EXTINCTION (activity dies and falls into absorbing state)

LATTICE + TIME DIRECTION
DP is defined on a space-time lattice:

    time →
    t=0:   . X . . .
    t=1:   X X . X .
    t=2:   . X X . X
    t=3:   . . X X .
    t=4:   . . . . .

Activity flows only forward in time. No backward influence.

SPACETIME VIEW

    t=0:      X
             / \
    t=1:    X   X
           / \ / \
    t=2:    X X   .
             \ \
    t=3:       X X
                \
    t=4:          .

Where X = active, . = inactive.

STOCHASTIC UPDATE RULE
Each active site at time t attempts to activate neighbors at time t+1
with probability p.

    ACTIVE(t) --p--> ACTIVE(t+1)
    ACTIVE(t) --(1-p)--> INACTIVE(t+1)

Inactive sites remain inactive unless activated by neighbors.

LOCAL UPDATE

    neighbors at t:
        X . X

    update to t+1:
        each neighbor tries to activate the center with probability p

ABSORBING STATE
DP has a unique absorbing configuration:

    all sites inactive

Once the system enters this state, it cannot leave.

ABSORBING STATE
    t=0:   . X . X .
    t=1:   . . X . .
    t=2:   . . . . .
    t=3:   . . . . .   <-- absorbing

PHASE TRANSITION
There is a critical probability p_c such that:

    p < p_c   → activity dies (absorbing phase)
    p > p_c   → activity survives (active phase)

Near p_c, the system exhibits power-law behavior and universal scaling.

PHASE DIAGRAM (conceptual)

    activity density
        ^
        |   active phase
        |      /
        |     /
        |    /
        |   /
        |  /
        | /
        |/__________________ p
             p_c

CRITICAL CLUSTER GEOMETRY
At p = p_c, clusters of activity form fractal-like structures in
space-time.

CRITICAL CLUSTER
    t=0:       X
    t=1:      X .
    t=2:     X X X
    t=3:      X   X
    t=4:       X X
    t=5:        X
    t=6:         .

Clusters are sparse, branching, and marginally surviving.

UNIVERSALITY
Directed percolation is the universal behavior for systems with:

    - a single absorbing state
    - short-range interactions
    - no special symmetries
    - stochastic activation/spreading
    - continuous phase transition

Many epidemic models, forest-fire PCA, and contact processes fall into
DP universality.

CONTACT PROCESS CONNECTION
The contact process is the simplest DP model:

    infected → infect neighbors with rate λ
    infected → recover with rate 1

Critical λ_c marks the DP transition.

